  /**
   * Level 41 — where the arrow lock is introduced.
   *
   * ⚠ A **teaching board sitting in a real slot**. It is off the tuned curve like every hand-built
   * level, and it is deliberately gentle (best play 93%, careless 77%) because its job is to make
   * one rule legible, not to be hard.
   *
   * The whole board is peeled from the bottom up and nothing else is in the way. The last row is
   * the only thing that can be poured at the start — it sits on the mouth of the chute, so it is
   * the only row with a way out — and every arrow above it points down at the row below, so each
   * pour wakes exactly one tray. The one arrow pointing **left** is there so the piece is not
   * mistaken for "arrows always mean down".
   *
   * The top row carries no arrows at all and still cannot be tapped at the start: it is hemmed in
   * on all four sides. That is the ordinary escape rule, and having it next to the locks is the
   * point — the player gets to see the two reasons a tray sits flat, in the same board.
   *
   * ⚠ **It is deliberately the smallest board for miles** — 12 trays against 30 on level 40 and
   * 21 on the board that used to be here. That is the trade: a new piece needs a board with
   * nothing else going on, and level 40 next door is a `hard` spike, so the breather lands well.
   */
